    "case_id": "337",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 40s presented with a 10-year history of progressive skin thickening and subcutaneous enlargement of the right side of her nose and right cheek. She had been seen by several dermatologists and otolaryngologists and had undergone nondiagnostic skin biopsies. She underwent a septorhinoplasty 6 months prior due to collapse of her right nasal ala. During surgery, significant submucosal tissue was noted and removed from the right nasal cavity. She initially improved but noticed enlargement of soft tissue with thickening of the overlying skin on her right cheek and right side of her nose several months later.\n\nClinical examination revealed slightly hyperplastic soft tissue with dilated patulous follicles involving the right medial malar cheek and right nasal ala in addition to deviation of the nasal tip.\n\n- Surgical Pathology:    1. Initial septorhinoplasty specimens showed fibroadipose tissue and vascular proliferation    2. Initial skin biopsy was interpreted as sebaceous hyperplasia    3. Review of surgical specimens revealed:       - Subcutaneous-based proliferation of spindle cells with varying density       - Thickened and fragmented collagen bundles       - Mature adipocytes extending into skeletal tissue       - Spindle cells had bland cytomorphology with tapered nuclei and ill-defined cytoplasm       - No cellular pleomorphism observed  - Images:    Image Title: Clinical image    Image Description: Shows facial asymmetry with deviation of the nasal tip    Image Title: Hematoxylin-eosin stain    Image Description: Shows interstitial proliferation of spindle cells within mature adipocytes extending between bundles of skeletal muscle    Image Title: Hematoxylin-eosin stain    Image Description: Shows mature adipocytes separated by proliferation of spindle cells associated with fragments of thickened collagen",
